<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    **Everything the Doctor does lives here. Only its window and its entry point live in the
    BloomFreezeDoctor project.**

    The reason is testing, and it is a hard boundary rather than a preference: BloomFreezeDoctor is a
    WinExe with UseWindowsForms whose output goes into Bloom's own folder with the target framework
    stripped off the path, and a test project cannot sensibly reference that. Code on that side of the
    line can only ever be exercised by running the application; code on this side can be unit-tested.

    So the rule for a new file: it belongs in BloomFreezeDoctor only if it is Windows Forms, or the
    process entry point. Everything else belongs here - including the parts that talk to processes,
    files and the network. Note what this project is therefore NOT: it is not "the pure logic". It holds
    WindowsTargetProbe, ReportOutbox and YouTrackSubmitter, which do all three. Detection logic is where
    the subtle mistakes live (see docs/SPIKE-FINDINGS.md) and must be testable without a frozen Bloom to
    hand, but that is an argument for keeping such code here, not for keeping anything else out.

    DoctorSupervisor is why this note exists. It sat in the exe for no better reason than that it was
    written there, putting 1,257 lines of ordinary logic - and every type declared alongside it - out of
    reach of the tests. That is how testing RestartBlockers came to require moving a type first.

    Both projects use RootNamespace BloomFreezeDoctor, so moving a file across the line changes no
    `using` and no call site anywhere.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
    <RootNamespace>BloomFreezeDoctor</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>BloomFreezeDoctor.Core</AssemblyName>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      So the tests can reach `internal` members. Used sparingly, for helpers whose behaviour has a stated
      consequence somewhere else - SafeFileName's null-versus-empty, for instance, which decides whether
      an Event Log search matches the right process or every message ever written.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="BloomFreezeDoctor.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The wire format Bloom and the Doctor share. Its own project so that Bloom can reference just the
      wire format without dragging in the Doctor's diagnostics - ClrMD, the AWS SDK, the Event Log
      reader - none of which Bloom has any use for.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BloomFreezeDoctor.Protocol\BloomFreezeDoctor.Protocol.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    **Every package here that BloomExe also uses must be pinned to the SAME version BloomExe uses.**
    Not a tidiness rule - the Doctor is installed into Bloom's own folder, which can hold only one copy
    of any DLL. Disagree, and one of the two runs against a version its .deps.json never named.

    It shows up first as a broken build. A running Doctor holds its DLLs open, and MSBuild skips copying
    a file that is already identical - so a matched dependency is invisible, while a mismatched one is
    rewritten on every Bloom build and fails with MSB3027 naming BloomFreezeDoctor. System.Management
    was at 8.0.0 against BloomExe's 9.0.7 and did exactly that; nothing else collided, because nothing
    else differed.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Reading the Windows Event Log for crash and unexpected-shutdown entries (plan 4.4). -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Diagnostics.EventLog" Version="8.0.1" />
    <!-- Reading another process's command line, to spot headless runs and the WebView2 debug port. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Management" Version="9.0.7" />
    <!-- Walking managed stacks out of a dump, or out of a live process without suspending it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Diagnostics.Runtime" Version="3.1.512801" />
    <!-- Asking the target's own runtime to write its dump, over the diagnostics IPC pipe. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Diagnostics.NETCore.Client" Version="0.2.510501" />
    <!--
      libpalaso, for SIL.IO.RobustFile and RobustIO. Available because the Doctor now lives in Bloom's
      repository, where this is already restored - and worth taking for a concrete reason rather than for
      consistency: the outbox's staging-to-final rename was losing a race against whatever else on Windows
      had just touched the files it wrote, failing about one run in three and discarding a gathered report.
      RobustIO retries; see the note at that call.

      Keep the version in step with BloomExe's SIL.* references by hand for now. If this layout is adopted,
      it belongs in Directory.Build.props alongside VelopackVersion, which already exists to be the single
      source of truth for exactly this kind of thing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="SIL.Core.Desktop" Version="18.0.0-beta0028" />
    <!--
      Not used here: SIL.Core.Desktop drags it in, so it lands in the folder either way. Pinned only to
      match BloomExe, which asks for beta0005 while our transitive resolution settled on beta0004 - the
      second half of the mismatch described above.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="L10NSharp" Version="10.0.0-beta0005" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
